# Java/Kotlin + Spring patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Spring Boot 3.x design, for both Java and Kotlin. Applies only to
`*.java`/`*.kt`/`*.kts` files.

## Transaction boundaries and the self-invocation pitfall

- `@Transactional` on the outermost service method that defines the unit of
  work, not on every repository call.
- A call to another `@Transactional` method on the same class through `this`
  bypasses the proxy and the annotation is ignored. Move the method to a
  separate constructor-injected bean.
- No unrelated I/O (HTTP calls, file access) inside a `@Transactional`
  method.

## Spring Data JPA query patterns

- N+1 on lazy associations: fix with `@EntityGraph`, JPQL `JOIN FETCH`, or a
  projection; no blanket `FetchType.EAGER`.
- A derived query method or `@Query` with named parameters over
  string-concatenated JPQL/native SQL.
- A projection (interface or DTO) for a read path needing a subset of an
  entity's fields.

## Dependency injection and bean design

- An interface at the consumer boundary only when a component has more than
  one implementation or must be mocked across module boundaries; none for a
  single Spring-managed implementation "for testability".
- Constructor injection producing an immutable, fully-initialized bean over
  setter or field injection.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "god service" accumulating unrelated business logic across domains.
- Catching a broad `Exception`/`RuntimeException` in a service only to log
  and swallow it, instead of propagating to a `@ControllerAdvice`/
  `@ExceptionHandler`.
- A `@RestController` method containing business/query logic instead of
  delegating to a service.
